{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes ads shown inside the game (rewarded or interstitial video ads in Township) that cannot be dismissed: there is no close button, tapping anywhere redirects to the store, and returning to the game leaves the ad screen stuck, forcing the player to kill the app. Two mechanics are present. First, the player is compelled to sit through advertisements with no way to exit, which is forced ad viewing. Second, the interface obstructs the opt-out: the close control is absent and any tap acts as a store redirect, meaning the dismissal control is effectively removed and interaction is routed toward a purchase surface. The 'stuck' phrasing could suggest a glitch, but the design described (no close mark, tap-to-store) is presented as how the ads behave, and the underlying behaviour is a forced, undismissable ad with a misdirecting tap target rather than a technical fault.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "recently after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it. No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player is compelled to view in-game advertisements with no means of dismissal, satisfying 'forced to watch ads'."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "there is no way to close it. No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Attention-directing without an opt-out (second arm)",
      "rationale": "The close control is absent and any tap routes to the store instead of dismissing, obstructing the opt-out and redirecting interaction toward a purchase surface."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer's main point is a feature request: they want the game to save progress and purchases so nothing is lost on deletion. Under R12, a suggested feature does not itself trigger any label. However, alongside the suggestion the reviewer describes an existing mechanic: their accumulated in-game purchases and hard work would be forfeited if they deleted the game, and that prospect is what keeps them attached ('I really don't want that to happen'). This is accumulated investment raising the cost of leaving, i.e., Invested/Endowed Value. Unlike the counterexample where the player had already lost the account and was disengaging, here the investment is actively operating on the reviewer's choice to stay. Nothing else in the review describes a mechanic: no timers, no ads, no payment gate described as currently functioning.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "I know if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value",
      "rationale": "The reviewer's accumulated purchases and progress make exit costly; the fear of forfeiting that investment is what keeps them from deleting the game, which is invested value operating on the decision to leave."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review is a glowing overview of Lords Mobile. Most of it is praise for graphics, storyline, and strategy depth, which are quality evaluations with no mechanic described. The one concrete mechanic named is real-time combat against other players: 'the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat,' which describes the game pitting players against each other. That matches Competition's definition directly. No monetary, temporal, or other psychological mechanic is described: nothing about purchases, timers, daily rewards, ads, or collection systems appears in the text.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition / Alt. def.: 'The game makes you compete against other players.'",
      "rationale": "The game places players in direct real-time combat against one another, which is competition by definition; assigned irrespective of the reviewer's positive framing (R7)."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

