{
  "analysis": "The reviewer praises the tactical design but describes one concrete mechanic: the game runs on an energy system, and once energy runs out progression stops until a fairly long real-time recovery timer refills it. That is the Wait to Play indicator: an in-game timer imposing an arbitrary wait that gates progress. The rest of the review (console version, storyline, customization) consists of wishes and hypothetical additions, which R12 excludes. No purchases, ads, social features, or rewards systems are described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "It's an Energy based game so once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully.",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play [low-level]: in-game timers that make you arbitrarily wait for something; boundary rule: Wait to Play requires the span to describe the wait or its regeneration schedule (energy refilling over time)",
      "rationale": "Energy depletes and blocks all progress until a long real-world recovery timer refills it, an explicit wait-to-play gate tied to progression."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review contains two distinct strands. First, the reviewer describes buying poke balls with real money and then observing that captures fail more often, forcing them to burn through balls faster and repurchase: an allegation that the game tunes outcomes to drive purchases, i.e., concealed manipulation of results. The payment also directly buys the means of continued play, and the paid purchases name an in-game item bought with real money, though no currency conversion is described, so no Intermediate Currency. Second, the edit reveals that the review itself was written and posted under the player's name without their knowledge: the game published content as the player. The boundary rule on Friend Spam / Impersonation explicitly covers a store review written under the player's account.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer asserts the game engineers capture failures once purchases begin, i.e., outcomes manipulated on a hidden schedule to drive more purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Continued play depends on repeatedly buying the consumable items that enable captures, which the game is alleged to deplete deliberately."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message boundary rule",
      "rationale": "A review was published under the player's name without their authorship, an act performed through the player's own account identity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer complains that levels take a week to pass and that tasks are impossible unless boosters are purchased. The first part describes slow progress, but does not name a specific repetitive activity the player must perform (farming, replaying, a task cycle), so under the Grinding vs Slow Progress boundary it does not qualify as Grinding. The second part directly states a pay wall: tasks cannot be completed without buying boosters, which is Pay to Progress — payment is presented as the required route past an obstacle. No other mechanic (timers, currencies, chance systems, social features) is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall [low-level]",
      "rationale": "Tasks are stated to be impossible without purchasing boosters, making payment the condition for progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer praises the game and compares its monetization favorably to other games. The key mechanic described is spending real money ($20) on items/purchases in the game, with bingo giving 'twice the number of your purchase' — this describes real money being converted into in-game currency/credits whose exchange rate obscures the true cost, which is Intermediate Currency (Premium Currency). The mention of 'different modes and events' names events but describes no schedule, expiry, or pressure operating on the player, so no temporal or FOMO label. Payment is never tied to progression being blocked or slowed, so Pay to Progress does not apply. The comparison about getting more for your money is a value judgment on pricing, not anchoring or a purchase-friction mechanism, so no Easy to Purchase.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "If your spend $20 on some item you get less then what your actually hoping for, but on here bingo gives you twice the number of your purchase",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency: 'Exchange rate between real money and in-game currency disguises the real price of items.'",
      "rationale": "Real money ($20) is exchanged for in-game purchase credit whose amount ('twice the number of your purchase') is expressed through the game's own conversion rather than a direct price, establishing the money-to-currency conversion."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a limited-time event in MONOPOLY GO! They were close to completing it (3 tasks left toward a 525-dice reward), ran out of dice, and saw the event carried a '2 Days left' timer. When they logged back in after sleeping, the event had changed and their progress toward the reward was gone. Two mechanics are active: (1) a game-set clock governing when the event and its rewards are available, with rewards forfeited when the window closes — the schedule operates on the player and is tied to reward loss, which is Playing by Appointment. (2) A partially completed goal whose near-completion kept the player engaged and whose loss is the felt harm — Endowed Progress, the partially-completed-goal arm of Easy to Get, Hard to Lose. The F2P/trust comments express general dissatisfaction with monetization but describe no specific purchase mechanic, so no monetary label. Running out of dice is a resource constraint but no regeneration timer or wait is described, so no Wait to Play beyond the event clock already coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; boundary rule vs Fear of missing out (reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule and the loss)",
      "rationale": "The event runs on a countdown set by the game and the reward was forfeited when the window closed, forcing play within the game's schedule or losing the reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice",
      "rule_applied": "Endowed Progress ('Badges / Endowed Progress')",
      "rationale": "The reviewer was three tasks from the reward and frames the near-complete progress as the wasted investment, the partially completed goal the design uses to hold engagement."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes three mechanics. First, the game rewards inviting friends and getting them to join via Facebook — recruitment of new players for in-game rewards, even though the reviewer complains the rewards stopped; the mechanic is still described as present in the game's design (R7/R8 code the mechanic, not the evaluation of its payout). Second, dice are the stake in a chance-based loop: the reviewer played max bet and got no shut downs or bank heists, describing a randomized outcome system — but no real money is mentioned, so this codes the variable reward delivery, not Gambling. Third, sticker drops are all duplicates, i.e., repeated unwanted drops from a randomised pool, which is Reward Mania delivery language; the reviewer does not describe compulsion to complete the album itself, so Complete the Collection is not assigned. The login failure is a technical fault (R11/R2) and codes nothing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "Suddenly I get no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme",
      "rationale": "The game rewards players for inviting friends to join, which instantiates the Social Pyramid Scheme indicator; the complaint is that the reward stopped, but the recruitment mechanic is described."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists. Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "Unpredictable outcomes from dice betting and duplicate drops from a randomised sticker pool describe chance-based reward delivery sustaining repeated attempts; no monetary stake is described, so Gambling does not apply."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a deliberate interface behaviour in 8 Ball Pool: on opening the game or the 1v1 section, the UI auto-scrolls to and pre-selects the highest-stakes table the player's coin balance allows. They call it a 'Dark UX' trick designed to cause accidental mis-clicks so players lose their coin balance and then have to buy more. Three mechanics follow: (1) a purchase action defaulted by the interface with accidental-spend intent, which is Easy to Purchase (Default To Purchase / Accidental Purchases); (2) the interface directing the player toward a selected option against their intent, which is UI Misdirection; (3) the implied real-money purchase of replacement coins ('forced to buy real ones'), where coins are the object bought with money, which is Intermediate Currency. The betting itself is ordinary 8 Ball Pool match wagering, not a chance-based purchase, so Gambling does not apply. The 'rigged' accusation is about the UI defaults, not about manipulated match outcomes, so Illusion of Control does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins. This is a cheap \"Dark UX\" trick to force accidental mis-clicks so players lose all their hard-earned coins",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases; Low Barrier / Default To Purchase",
      "rationale": "The game pre-selects the highest-stakes table as the default action, engineering accidental entry into a large spend with no opt-out step; the player must actively change the selection to avoid it."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "the user interface is completely rigged. Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table",
      "rule_applied": "definition: interface focuses attention on specific options against the player's intent",
      "rationale": "Auto-scrolling to and highlighting the highest-bet table is the interface directing attention and selection toward the option that benefits the game at the player's expense."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "so players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Purchases denominated in currency boundary rule: currency is the object acquired",
      "rationale": "Coins are bought as replacements for lost ones, i.e. real money converted into the in-game currency, establishing the conversion relation."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes three mechanics. First, an assertion that the randomness is tuned against the player: 'the rng is so obviously against you' claims the game conceals or manipulates how outcomes are determined, which is Illusion of Control rather than a mere losing streak, since a rigged-RNG claim is made. Second, the free-versus-paid contrast: playing without spending actual money is 'not rewarding', meaning smooth advancement effectively requires payment, which is Pay to Progress. Third, the level economics: at around level 100 each attempt costs 2200 coins while a win barely recoups that stake. This describes the currency being spent within the game economy, but the review never shows real money being converted into coins, so Intermediate Currency's transaction-direction rule is not satisfied. The coin drain per level is part of the pay-to-progress pressure (payment becomes the route to keep playing), not a separate currency conversion, and no wait timer, reward schedule, or other pattern is described. No search is needed; the mechanics are clear from the text.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The span asserts the game manipulates the determination of outcomes via tuned RNG, which the boundary rule codes as Illusion of Control rather than a mere unlucky run."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's not rewarding playing the game without spending actual money.. at around level 100 it costs 2200 but if you win the level you'll barely make that back",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The 'without spending actual money' contrast plus per-level costs exceeding winnings establishes that the free route falls short of smooth advancement, making payment the practical path to keep progressing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

